    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d436">

                <pb facs="0439" n="427" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg427"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War, 1939/332: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Rumania</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gunther</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bucharest</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-16T09:00:00+03:00">September 16,
                            1939—9 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 1:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>198. Following from Biddle:<anchor n="40" xml:id="d436-f1"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Series Z3. September 14, 6 p.m. My confidential conversation with
                                Beck<anchor n="41" xml:id="d436-f2"/> yesterday morning reveals
                            following: (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) Germans had destroyed almost
                            all Polish war industry; (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) situation now
                            extremely difficult, nevertheless Beck joined Marshal Smigly-Rydz<anchor
                                n="42" xml:id="d436-f3"/> in emphasizing that Polish Army would
                            defend even the last foot of Polish soil; (<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >c</hi>) impression rapidly gaining ground among military and other
                            official circles that Great Britain and France are staging more of a
                            demonstration than a serious attack; (<hi rendition="#italic">d</hi>)
                            Beck’s current reports indicate that official circles in London and
                            Paris state that <pb facs="0440" n="428" xml:id="pg428"/> British and
                            French planes hesitate to bomb German railways, other communications,
                            power plants and war industry centers because of potential effect on
                            American public opinion (another ranking army officer interjected that
                            if the French and British hesitated to bombard along these lines why did
                            they not send airplanes for Polish pilots to carry out the disagreeable
                            task. For the latter it would be a case of justified retaliation). Beck
                            concluded by emphasizing that this was a dramatic moment for
                            Poland—perhaps a matter even of Poland’s life or death.</p>
                        <p>I discern that both the Foreign Minister and the Marshal feel that if the
                            French and British do not embark immediately upon a major shock attack
                            it may spell the end of Poland.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gunther</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d436-f1" n="40" xml:id="d436-f1-content">
                <p>Anthony J. Drexel Biddle, Jr., American Ambassador to Poland. For correspondence
                    concerning the evacuation from Poland of the American Mission, see <ref
                        target="frus1939v02">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>
                    </ref>, section entitled “Evacuation of the American Embassy Staff …” under
                    Poland.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d436-f2" n="41" xml:id="d436-f2-content">
                <p>Józef Beck, Polish Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d436-f3" n="42" xml:id="d436-f3-content">
                <p>Marshal Edward Smigly-Rydz, Inspector General of the Polish Army.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
